Strategy: // CSD_RATIONALE_BEGIN
// Spider text-to-SQL typically has a long unconstrained preamble (schema,
// question, explanatory text) followed by one SQL query that should be grammar-
// valid under the schema-specific parser. This strategy therefore tracks only
// the active constrained SQL span (`currentConstrainedOut`) plus whether we are
// currently inside that span. Outside the span it uses chunked unconstrained
// generation to move quickly until either EOS or the explicit open delimiter
// "<<" appears. Inside the span it adapts to parser narrowness: when few next
// tokens are valid, it uses tight token-level constrained decoding; when many
// continuations are valid, it uses symbol-level constrained decoding so common
// SQL fragments (keywords, identifiers, short expressions) can be emitted in a
// more natural multi-token unit.
//
// The observations used to choose actions are: whether we are already inside a
// constrained span, whether the current constrained prefix is complete, and the
// parser's valid-next count at the current SQL prefix. This tracked state is
// sufficient for validity because the parser only ever sees the constrained SQL
// content between delimiters; outside the span we do not claim SQL validity,
// and inside the span every append is either parser-validated token-by-token or
// produced by ConstrainedSymbol, which returns a valid parser prefix.
// CSD_RATIONALE_END
// CSD_PROOF_SKETCH_BEGIN
