Strategy: // CSD_RATIONALE_BEGIN
// The previous strategy overused ConstrainedSymbol in wide SQL states. On Spider,
// those are exactly the places where the grammar admits many locally valid but
// semantically drifting continuations (extra joins, aliases, unfinished ON
// clauses). That produced long syntactically valid yet incorrect queries and a
// few repetition loops. The revised strategy therefore becomes much more
// conservative inside the constrained SQL span: it decodes one token at a time,
// but biases the logits based on parser state to encourage early, complete SQL
// queries instead of maximal valid continuations.
//
// Concretely, once inside `<< ... >>`, the strategy always calls a helper that
// generates logits for the constrained context, then adjusts logits before
// sampling. If the current SQL prefix is complete, it strongly boosts the close
// delimiter `>>` and penalizes common continuation tokens such as JOIN, ON,
// WHERE, GROUP, ORDER, HAVING, LIMIT, commas, and alias markers, reducing the
// tendency to keep extending already-good queries. If the parser is in a narrow
// state, it boosts the top valid candidates to stay decisive. If dead-end risk
// is detected, it boosts only a few top valid candidates more aggressively.
// Outside the constrained span, it still uses chunked unconstrained generation
// to quickly reach `<<` or EOS.
//
// This change directly targets the observed failures: unfinished long joins are
// reduced by preferring closure whenever the SQL prefix is complete, and local
// repetition is reduced by penalizing extension-heavy structural tokens once a
// valid query already exists. The tracked state remains only the full generated
// output plus the active constrained suffix between delimiters.
// CSD_RATIONALE_END
// CSD_PROOF_SKETCH_BEGIN
// parser_validity: In the unconstrained chunk branch, we either remain outside
//   the span or enter on `<<` with `currentConstrainedOut := []`; the latter is
//   valid by the method precondition `parser.IsValidPrefix([])`. In the
//   complete-prefix close branch, CloseConstrainedSpan returns outside-span
//   state, so the implication is vacuous. In the token-level constrained branch,
//   we only append a token chosen after masking valid next tokens (or break on
//   EOS); AppendConstrainedToken preserves parser validity for the resulting
//   constrained prefix.
//
// suffix: In the unconstrained chunk branch, staying outside makes the
//   implication vacuous; entering on `<<` sets `currentConstrainedOut := []`,
//   and the length-0 suffix of `generated` is `[]`. In the close branch,
//   CloseConstrainedSpan resets `insideConstrainedOut` to false, so the
//   implication is vacuous. In the token-level constrained branch,
//   AppendConstrainedToken appends the same token to both `generated` and the
//   constrained suffix, so the trailing suffix remains exactly
//   `currentConstrainedOut`.
//
// cost accounting: UnconstrainedChunk returns `stepsUsed`, and we add exactly
//   that to `steps`. CloseConstrainedSpan consumes one generation step, so we
//   increment `steps` by 1. In the constrained token branch we call
//   `lm.ChooseNextToken()` exactly once after masking, manually bump
//   `helpers.cost` by 1, and increment `steps` by 1. No branch decreases
//   `steps`; `cost` stays 0 throughout the loop and is assigned `steps` after
//   the loop, so returned cost is bounded by `maxSteps`.
//
// progress: UnconstrainedChunk appends at most `stepsUsed` tokens and we
//   increase `steps` by `stepsUsed`, preserving
//   `|generated| <= |generatedPrefix| + steps`. CloseConstrainedSpan appends one
//   token and increments `steps` by 1. In the constrained token branch, either
//   EOS is chosen and we break after incrementing `steps` by 1 without changing
//   `generated`, or we append exactly one token via AppendConstrainedToken and
//   also increment `steps` by 1. All arithmetic is linear, so the invariant is
//   preserved in every branch.
// CSD_PROOF_SKETCH_END
